description:
Write the conversions that `ConvertSidToStringSidW` and `ConvertStringSidToSidW` perform, and read an integrity level (lesson 7.05).

```cpp
struct Sid { std::uint64_t authority; std::vector<std::uint32_t> subAuthorities; };

std::vector<std::uint8_t> sidToBytes(const Sid& sid);
std::optional<Sid> sidFromBytes(const std::vector<std::uint8_t>& bytes);
std::string sidToString(const Sid& sid);
std::optional<Sid> sidFromString(std::string_view text);
std::string integrityName(const Sid& sid);
```

**The binary form**: byte 0 is the revision, 1; byte 1 is the number of sub-authorities (at most 15); bytes 2 to 7 are the identifier authority, a 48-bit number, **big-endian**; then each sub-authority as 4 bytes, **little-endian**. `sidFromBytes` refuses (empty optional) a wrong revision, more than 15 sub-authorities, or a length that is not exactly `8 + 4 × count`. `sidToBytes` may assume a valid `Sid` (authority below 2⁴⁸, at most 15 sub-authorities).

**The text form**: `S-1-`, the authority, then `-` and each sub-authority in decimal. The authority is written in decimal if it is below 2³², otherwise as `0x` followed by 12 upper-case hex digits. `sidFromString` accepts exactly that form (the `S` may be lower case, hex digits in either case), with each number made of digits only, the authority below 2⁴⁸, each sub-authority below 2³², and at most 15 of them; anything else gives an empty optional.

**integrityName**: for a SID with authority 16 and exactly one sub-authority, the name of the level: `untrusted` (0), `low` (0x1000), `medium` (0x2000), `medium plus` (0x2100), `high` (0x3000), `system` (0x4000), otherwise `unknown`. Any other SID gives `not an integrity level`.

